# The ClangSharp option set that turns core/include/mxq.h into the committed C#
# declarations in MiniXiangqi.Core/Generated/Mxq.g.cs. The generator version is
# pinned in windows/.config/dotnet-tools.json and is stamped into the output's
# [assembly: GeneratedCode]. Input and output paths are not here: generate.ps1
# passes them absolute, so this file is a recipe rather than a location.
#
# One option per line. Lines beginning with # are comments.

# mxq.h is plain C by contract, and reading it as C++ would change name
# mangling assumptions and admit C++ constructs the header promises not to use.
--language
c

# The C# side of the boundary.
--namespace
MiniXiangqi.Core.Interop

# The static class the 54 functions and every named constant land on.
--method-class-name
Mxq

# The DllImport library name. This resolves mxqcore.dll beside the consuming
# assembly on Windows, and is deliberately undecorated: the loader appends the
# platform's own prefix and suffix.
--library-path
mxqcore

# .NET 10 / C# 14 output: C# 12 InlineArray for the header's fixed-capacity char
# arrays, and unmanaged function pointers for MxqSearchCallback. The older
# codegen levels spell both as `fixed` buffers and delegates, which are the two
# places a hand-written binding would stop being blittable.
--config
codegen=latest
--config
file=single
--generate
file-scoped-namespaces

# The capacity, version and budget constants mxq.h defines as macros —
# MXQ_FEN_CAP, MXQ_API_VERSION_MAJOR, MXQ_ENGINE_MIN_HASH_MIB and the rest —
# rather than leaving the C# side to restate numbers the header owns.
--generate
macro-bindings

# [assembly: DisableRuntimeMarshalling]. The interface is blittable by
# construction; this says so to the runtime, so no P/Invoke in the bindings
# assembly can quietly acquire a marshalling stub.
--generate
disable-runtime-marshalling
